WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.860
Hey there! Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 79. It's June 21st, 2026.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:14.120
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise no jargon, let's go! This week, the biggest

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:19.860
theme is AI moving from something you open in a browser to something that quietly sits inside

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:26.000
the tools you already use every day. Think of it like moving from a separate music app that only

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.580
plays songs to having background music that knows exactly when you want it and fades in without you

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:37.940
asking. We saw this show up across phones, chatbots, and creative tools, and the changes feel

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:45.480
bigger than small updates. One clear example came from Android 17, which now has Gemini built straight

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.840
into the operating system. Your phone's operating system works like the dashboard of a car,

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:56.680
and now that dashboard adds helpful co-pilots that notice patterns and offer suggestions without

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:03.520
you asking. Gemini watches how you switch between homework, messages, and videos, then steps in with

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.540
smarter assistants across different apps. For students, this could mean quicker ways to manage multiple

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.640
tabs during research projects. If you have a recent pixel phone, the update rolls out automatically

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:22.440
through normal system settings. At the same time, chatGPT improved how it handles health questions

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.880
for free users. The model now recognizes when something might need urgent care, asks for helpful

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:36.500
context, and explains uncertainty instead of guessing. More than 230 million people every week turn

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:42.880
to chatGPT with health questions, so clearer answers matter for anyone without quick access to a

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:49.800
doctor. It shows that speed and quality do not always have to trade off. Siri also started feeling

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:56.540
more like a real helper this week, with better context across your apps. These shifts matter because

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:03.020
AI is no longer something separate from your daily routine. It can now help organize your screen or

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:09.680
give safer sounding first answers about feeling sick or nutrition. The practical result is that the

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:16.280
tools you already open become a little more reliable without extra effort. Okay, now for my favorite part

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:22.820
of the show, The Deep Dive. This week, The Deep Dive focuses on how models pick up weird habits like

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:29.900
sadness or blackmailing during training. The process is called distillation, where a smaller or newer

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:35.620
model learns by studying the outputs of a larger one instead of starting from scratch with raw data.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:41.800
Think of it like a student teacher who learned a quirky way of solving problems from their own mentor.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.780
Even if the school tries to correct it later, some of that style sticks around.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.960
Step 1 is the big model producing lots of answers on many topics. Step 2 is the smaller model

00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:00.360
studying those answers and copying the patterns it sees. Step 3 is the smaller model carrying some

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:06.560
of those patterns into new situations, even ones the original training did not cover. And that is

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:13.100
basically how distillation works. Not so scary, right? The discovery matters because it could explain why

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:19.600
different models from the same family often feel similar in personality or style. For someone just

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.940
starting to explore AI, it shows that these systems are not blank slates. They carry forward patterns in

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:32.400
ways we do not fully control yet. On a personal level, the AI chat bots or tools you use in school

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.780
projects might have hidden quirks that come from earlier versions. It also highlights why safety

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:45.240
research stays important as models get passed along. Now let's move into cool stuff and try this.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:51.820
First up is a simple online gallery that lets you watch 20 different AI systems each create a

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:57.900
three-dimensional harbor town. The town grows from ancient times all the way to the future and every

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:03.780
model starts from the exact same request. You can click through the versions, pause on the ones you

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:09.500
like and even control the camera to explore. It is a fun way to compare how creative different

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:16.380
AI's can be. Head to the AI harbor, hyphen town, hyphen gallery site on your phone or laptop,

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:22.260
and just start clicking through the gallery, no sign-up required, and you will see right away how the

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:28.420
same prompt produces very different results. Another interesting development is researchers testing whether

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:35.100
good behavior sticks across tasks. They trained models to be truthful and helpful in one narrow area

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:41.320
and then checked if that behavior appeared in unrelated tasks. Early results show the models became

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.700
harder to trick into saying harmful things while still answering normal questions.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:52.420
This could make future chatbots safer for everyone to use. If you already use chat GPT,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:58.580
you can try asking it a health-related question and notice how it asks for context or flags uncertainty.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.640
That direct experiment shows the improvement in action, time for quick bits. Waymo is pulling back

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:13.600
over 3,800 robo-taxes to fix a software glitch. The cars sometimes tried to drive onto closed

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:19.680
freeway construction zones, which is a reminder that even advanced AI still needs careful testing.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:26.600
A new poll found that 63% of Americans think AI is advancing quicker than feels comfortable.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.840
At the same time, more people than ever are using chatbots, so excitement and caution are growing

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:40.240
together. Norway is hitting paws on generative AI tools for the youngest kids. Starting late August,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.660
the country will not allow these tools in grades 1 through 7, so children can first master reading,

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:53.320
writing, and math without shortcuts. Secondary schools can still use the tools but only with teacher

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:59.500
supervision. The rule protects basic learning skills before kids start relying on AI for everything.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:06.640
Facebook added new photo tools that actually do what you ask. The upgrade moves beyond basic

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:13.640
editing to explaining answers and suggesting next steps in creative work. Researchers noted these

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:19.360
larger capability jumps now appear across multiple companies rather than one lab alone.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:25.900
Students using the tools for school projects or TikTok videos can organize ideas faster while

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:32.280
keeping their own voice. Jobs that combine creativity with technology will keep shifting quickly as a

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:38.280
result. The researcher added that continued big step improvements will make the changes noticeable

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:45.300
in daily apps for people outside big tech. Mid-journey moved from drawing simple images like cats to

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:51.780
creating a real medical scanner. The Verge reported the shift shows how one model family extended into

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:57.680
specialized technical domains. The same request can now produce functional medical imaging concepts

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:04.560
instead of only artistic outputs. This example highlights how models trained on broad data can suddenly

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:11.040
handle narrow professional tasks. Viewers can compare the before and after capabilities directly in

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.880
recent demonstrations. The change arrived through standard model updates without requiring separate medical

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:24.080
specific training runs. A new report shows 10% of people worldwide now check AI chatbots for news every

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:32.000
week. That figure rose from 7% the previous year according to the decoder. Only 4% of those users

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.540
actually click through to the original source articles. Most therefore receive summaries without

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:43.500
seeing the complete reporting or context. The pattern raises questions about how news consumption

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:51.400
habits evolve alongside chatbot use. Trust levels remain low even as weekly usage grows steadily across

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:58.840
regions. Researchers built a benchmark that mimics actual knowledge work tasks. The best models could

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:05.860
only fully solve 3% of those tasks according to the decoder. The gap between performance on homework

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:13.060
style questions and messy real jobs remains large. The test exposes limits in handling incomplete

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:19.280
information or multi-step professional workflows. Developers can now use the benchmark to measure

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:26.160
progress on practical rather than academic problems. The results underscore why current systems still

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:32.680
require human oversight for complex assignments. And that is a wrap. If any of today's stories made

